\section{Reading the result}{

Start with \code{summary(ci)}, then inspect \code{ci$checks}, \code{ci$numerical_checks}
and \code{ci$cautions}. Keep these with the interval table when saving or sharing.
\link[=plot.mfrm_population_intervals]{plot(ci)} displays saved coefficients;
\code{plot(ci, type = "contrasts")} displays the supplied linear contrasts.
Unavailable intervals remain visible, and \code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}} reuses the same data.
\itemize{
\item \code{Status = "available"} and \code{CIEligible = TRUE} mean that the numerical
checks permit the experimental interval. They do not certify nominal
coverage. \code{InferenceReview} records this distinction in each row.
\item \code{Status = "fixed"} retains a supplied residual variance without an
inferential interval; missing uncertainty is not an estimated zero SE.
\item \code{Status = "numerical_review_failed"} retains the point estimate and
failed-check reason, with unavailable SEs and bounds. \code{nonpositive_variance}
or \code{nonfinite_bounds} instead identifies an unusable target-specific
uncertainty calculation. Missing bounds are not infinite endpoints.
\item \code{NaturalScaleStatus = "outside_floating_point_range"} means the finite
log-variance interval cannot be fully represented after exponentiation.
Inspect \code{WorkingLower} and \code{WorkingUpper}; a displayed zero is not an
interval qualified for a zero-variance boundary.
}

Coverage evidence is specific to the target, design and complete estimation
procedure. In the retained RSM checks, mean-at-\eqn{X=1} and log-variance
intervals at 20 Persons remain inconclusive under the prespecified practical
criteria. The independent 240-Person slope/mean-difference confirmation
supports those targets in its tested design; it does not establish a minimum
sample size or qualify arbitrary regressions, PCM designs or all contrasts.
All outputs from this helper therefore retain their experimental status.

These intervals do not add calibration uncertainty to individual scoring.
\code{\link[=predict_mfrm_units]{predict_mfrm_units()}} has separate source and scoring checks; its conditional
posterior intervals hold the fitted calibration and population parameters
fixed. Availability here does not authorize an otherwise refused scoring
result. See \code{\link[=mfrmr_interval_guide]{mfrmr_interval_guide()}} for the different uncertainty targets.
}

\section{Session plot defaults}{

Set \code{options(mfrmr.plot_preset = "publication")} to choose a session default
for plotting functions that expose the common \code{preset} argument. The
supported values are \code{"standard"}, \code{"publication"}, \code{"compact"} and
\code{"monochrome"}. Precedence is an explicit call argument, then the session
option, then \code{"standard"}. For example, \code{preset = "standard"} overrides
a session set to \code{"monochrome"}. Explicit \code{preset = NULL} retains the
earlier package-default behavior; it does not read the session option.
Invalid session values cause an error only when that option is needed.

The category-curve, data-quality, fit-review, connectivity and network
routes of \code{plot()} for report bundles use the same option through \code{...}.
Plots without a common \code{preset} argument, including extended-model plots
with their own \code{palette} controls, keep their own settings. This option
selects a preset, not a universal theme or a guarantee that all renderers
implement every appearance control identically.

New plot payloads retain the resolved preset for supported saved-data
rendering. Converting an existing payload with \code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}} uses its saved
appearance, even after the session option changes. A call that creates a
new plot from a fit or statistical result uses the current default.
For a reproducible script, supply \code{preset} explicitly or set the option in
that script. Saving only the fitted model does not save a session option.
No global ggplot theme is changed.

Restore previous settings with \code{old <- options(mfrmr.plot_preset = "monochrome")} followed by \code{options(old)}. Use
\code{options(mfrmr.plot_preset = NULL)} to remove the option. The preset changes
appearance, not estimates, confidence levels or diagnostic thresholds.
}

\examples{
# Long-running workflow; run explicitly when needed.
# example("mfrm_population_intervals", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_core")
persons <- data.frame(Person = unique(ratings$Person))
# Fictional predictor for this example; use your observed covariates.
persons$X <- seq(-1, 1, length.out = nrow(persons))
fit <- fit_mfrm(ratings, "Person", c("Rater", "Criterion"), "Score",
  model = "RSM", method = "MML", population_formula = ~ X,
  person_data = persons, quad_points = 61)
L <- matrix(c(0, 2), nrow = 1,
  dimnames = list("X = 1 minus X = -1", c("(Intercept)", "X")))
ci <- mfrm_population_intervals(fit, contrasts = L)
subset(summary(ci), Type == "coefficient_contrast")
plot(ci)
p <- plot(ci, type = "contrasts")
plot_data(p, "contrast_weights")
plot_data(p, "notes")
results <- mfrm_results(fit, intervals = list(population = ci),
  include = "fit", compute = "never")
apa_table(results$population_intervals$population)
}
}
